WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.170
The two building-block categories most relevant to TLS migration are key establishment and digital signatures.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.598
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM,

00:00:08.598 --> 00:00:13.049
helps two parties establish shared secret material over an untrusted channel.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:17.905
In post-quantum planning, ML-KEM is the main KEM concept teams will encounter.

00:00:17.905 --> 00:00:21.142
It is intended for key establishment use cases,

00:00:21.142 --> 00:00:24.784
not for signing certificates or authenticating software by itself.

00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:27.212
Digital signatures solve a different problem.

00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:32.067
They help a verifier confirm that data was signed by the holder

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:36.923
of a private key and that the signed data has not changed.

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:42.993
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA are post-quantum digital signature concepts practitioners may see in discussions about certificates,

00:00:42.993 --> 00:00:46.230
code signing, identity, service authentication, and long-term verification.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:51.491
They are not interchangeable with KEMs because they answer a different security question.

00:00:51.491 --> 00:00:53.514
That distinction matters for TLS.

00:00:53.514 --> 00:00:58.370
A deployment may experiment with post-quantum or hybrid key establishment before the

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.607
broader certificate ecosystem is ready for post-quantum signatures.

00:01:01.607 --> 00:01:05.249
Post-quantum certificates and certificate chains introduce separate compatibility concerns,

00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:09.700
such as signature size, chain size, client support, certificate authority readiness,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.151
browser behavior, operating system trust, embedded devices, and enterprise inspection tools.

00:01:14.151 --> 00:01:17.793
One migration track does not automatically solve the other.

00:01:17.793 --> 00:01:21.840
Teams should use standards-based algorithms through mature libraries and protocols.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.505
Custom TLS cryptography is dangerous because security depends on more than the algorithm name.

00:01:27.505 --> 00:01:31.956
It depends on negotiation behavior, transcript binding, downgrade resistance, error handling,

00:01:31.956 --> 00:01:34.788
implementation quality, side-channel considerations, interoperability, and maintenance.

00:01:34.788 --> 00:01:39.644
The practical advice is simple: do not design private post-quantum TLS constructions.

00:01:39.644 --> 00:01:44.500
Track reputable standards work, use maintained libraries, and test in realistic environments.
